{"item_uid": "指考100-english-021", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 36, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "Why do some people prefer using dogs than pigs in search of truffles?", "options": {"A": "Dogs have stronger paws to dig.", "B": "Dogs usually won’t eat the truffles found.", "C": "Dogs have a better sense of smell than pigs.", "D": "Dogs are less likely to get excited than pigs."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "B", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 8.0, "B": 72.0, "C": 15.0, "D": 5.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 1.0, "B": 97.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 1.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 16.0, "B": 38.0, "C": 36.0, "D": 9.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 72.0, "p_high": 97.0, "p_low": 38.0, "discrimination": 59.0, "p_quintiles": [98.0, 93.0, 81.0, 56.0, 30.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-022", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 37, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "What is the best way to enjoy truffles as a delicacy?", "options": {"A": "Eating them cooked with pork.", "B": "Eating them uncooked with bland oil.", "C": "Eating them fresh right after being collected.", "D": "Eating them after being refrigerated."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "C", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 2.0, "B": 13.0, "C": 78.0, "D": 6.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 98.0, "D": 1.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 6.0, "B": 29.0, "C": 51.0, "D": 14.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 78.0, "p_high": 98.0, "p_low": 51.0, "discrimination": 47.0, "p_quintiles": [99.0, 96.0, 87.0, 67.0, 42.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-023", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 38, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": {"A": "Truffles are roots of some old trees.", "B": "Truffles can be found only by dogs and pigs.", "C": "Truffles send out a strong odor when they mature.", "D": "Truffles cannot be collected at the same place repeatedly."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "C", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 10.0, "B": 15.0, "C": 65.0, "D": 11.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 3.0, "B": 6.0, "C": 87.0, "D": 3.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 15.0, "B": 23.0, "C": 42.0, "D": 19.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 65.0, "p_high": 87.0, "p_low": 42.0, "discrimination": 45.0, "p_quintiles": [92.0, 78.0, 65.0, 52.0, 36.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-024", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 39, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "Which of the following can be inferred from the passage?", "options": {"A": "Truffles sold in glass jars are tasteless.", "B": "Truffles taste like fruit when eaten fresh.", "C": "Truffles are only used for cooking nowadays.", "D": "Truffles are expensive because they are difficult to find.\n第第第第 40 至至至至 43 題題題題為為為為題題題題組組組組\nIn an ideal world, people would not test medicines on animals. Such experiments are stressful and\nsometimes painful for animals, and expensive and time-consuming for people. Yet animal\nexperimentation is still needed to help bridge vast gaps in medical knowledge. That is why there are some\n50 to 100 million animals used in research around the world each year.\nEurope, on the whole, has the world’s most restrictive laws on animal experiments. Even so, its\nscientists use some 12 million animals a year, most of them mice and rats, for medical research. Official\nstatistics show that just 1.1 million animals are used in research in America each year. But that is\n- 4 -\n第 5 頁 100年指考\n共 7 頁 英文考科\nmisleading. The American authorities do not think mice and rats are worth counting and, as these are the\nmost common laboratory animals, the true figure is much higher. Japan and China have even less\ncomprehensive data than America.\nNow Europe is reforming the rules governing animal experiments by restricting the number of\nanimals used in labs. Alternatives to animal testing, such as using human tissue or computer models, are\nnow strongly recommended. In addition, sharing all research results freely should help to reduce the\nnumber of animals for scientific use. At present, scientists often share only the results of successful\nexperiments. If their findings do not fit the hypothesis being tested, the work never sees the light of day.\nThis practice means wasting time, money, and animals’ lives in endlessly repeating the failed\nexperiments.\nAnimal experimentation has taught humanity a great deal and saved countless lives. It needs to\ncontinue, even if that means animals sometimes suffer. Europe’s new measures should eventually both\nreduce the number of animals used in experiments and improve the way in which scientific research is\nconducted."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "D", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 6.0, "B": 7.0, "C": 5.0, "D": 82.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 2.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 97.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 14.0, "B": 18.0, "C": 12.0, "D": 57.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 82.0, "p_high": 97.0, "p_low": 57.0, "discrimination": 40.0, "p_quintiles": [98.0, 96.0, 92.0, 79.0, 44.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-025", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 40, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "What is the main idea of this passage?", "options": {"A": "The success of animal experiments should be ensured.", "B": "Ban on the use of animals in the lab should be enforced.", "C": "Greater efforts need to be taken to reduce the number of lab animals.", "D": "Scientists should be required to share their research results with each other."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "C", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 8.0, "B": 21.0, "C": 61.0, "D": 10.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 3.0, "B": 10.0, "C": 83.0, "D": 4.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 15.0, "B": 26.0, "C": 40.0, "D": 17.0}, "omit_rate": 1.0, "p_value": 61.0, "p_high": 83.0, "p_low": 40.0, "discrimination": 43.0, "p_quintiles": [88.0, 73.0, 61.0, 50.0, 35.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-026", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 41, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "Which of the following statements is true about animals used in the lab?", "options": {"A": "America uses only about 1.1 million lab animals per year.", "B": "Europe does not use mice and rats as lab animals at all.", "C": "Britain does not use as many lab animals as China does.", "D": "Japan has limited data on the number of lab animals used each year."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "D", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 25.0, "B": 9.0, "C": 21.0, "D": 46.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 8.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 23.0, "D": 68.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 37.0, "B": 19.0, "C": 19.0, "D": 25.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 46.0, "p_high": 68.0, "p_low": 25.0, "discrimination": 43.0, "p_quintiles": [74.0, 57.0, 45.0, 33.0, 20.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-027", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 42, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "Which of the following is mentioned as an alternative to replace animal experiments?", "options": {"A": "Statistical studies.", "B": "Computer models.", "C": "DNA planted in animals.", "D": "Tissue from dead animals."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "B", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 7.0, "B": 75.0, "C": 10.0, "D": 8.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 1.0, "B": 98.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 1.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 16.0, "B": 42.0, "C": 23.0, "D": 19.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 75.0, "p_high": 98.0, "p_low": 42.0, "discrimination": 56.0, "p_quintiles": [98.0, 95.0, 87.0, 64.0, 31.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-028", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 43, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "What usually happens to unsuccessful animal experiments?", "options": {"A": "They are not revealed to the public.", "B": "They are made into teaching materials.", "C": "They are collected for future publication.", "D": "They are not removed from the research topic list.\n第第第第 44 至至至至 47 題題題題為為為為題題題題組組組組\nSpider webs are one of the most fascinating examples\nof animal architecture. The most beautiful and structurally\nordered are the orb webs. The main function of the web is\nto intercept and hold flying prey, such as flies, bees and\nother insects, long enough for the spider to catch them. In\norder to do so, the threads of the web have to withstand the\nimpact forces from large and heavy prey as well as\nenvironmental forces from wind and rain for at least a day\nin most cases.\n- 5 -\n100年指考 第 6 頁\n英文考科 共 7 頁\nThe orb web is found to have two main characteristics. The first is its geometry, which consists of an\nouter frame and a central part from which threads radiate outward. Enclosed in the frame are capture\nspirals winding round and round from the web center out to the frame. The whole web is in tension and\nheld in place by anchor threads, which connect the frame to the surrounding vegetation or objects. The\nsecond and perhaps most important characteristic is the material with which it is built. Spider silk is a\nkind of natural composite that gives this lightweight fiber a tensile strength comparable to that of steel,\nwhile at the same time making it very elastic. Two types of silk threads are used in the web. One is highly\nelastic and can stretch to almost twice its original length before breaking and, for most types of spiders, is\ncovered in glue. This type is used in the capture spiral for catching and holding prey. The other is stiffer\nand stronger, and is used for the radius, frames and anchor threads, which allows the web to withstand\nprey impact and to keep its structural strength through a wide range of environmental conditions."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "A", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 62.0, "B": 10.0, "C": 11.0, "D": 17.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 92.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 7.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 25.0, "B": 22.0, "C": 26.0, "D": 27.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 62.0, "p_high": 92.0, "p_low": 25.0, "discrimination": 67.0, "p_quintiles": [94.0, 86.0, 70.0, 41.0, 18.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-029", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 44, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "What is this passage mainly about?", "options": {"A": "The food network in nature.", "B": "The construction of orb webs.", "C": "The network of geometrical studies.", "D": "The environmental challenges for spider webs."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "B", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 3.0, "B": 74.0, "C": 7.0, "D": 15.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 0.0, "B": 98.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 1.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 8.0, "B": 43.0, "C": 14.0, "D": 35.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 74.0, "p_high": 98.0, "p_low": 43.0, "discrimination": 55.0, "p_quintiles": [99.0, 95.0, 83.0, 61.0, 34.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-030", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 45, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "What does the word “so” in the first paragraph refer to?", "options": {"A": "To catch and keep small creatures.", "B": "To find a good material for the web.", "C": "To observe the behavior patterns of spiders.", "D": "To present a fantastic architecture by animals."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "A", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 74.0, "B": 10.0, "C": 10.0, "D": 6.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 98.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 41.0, "B": 21.0, "C": 25.0, "D": 13.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 74.0, "p_high": 98.0, "p_low": 40.0, "discrimination": 58.0, "p_quintiles": [99.0, 95.0, 86.0, 64.0, 28.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-031", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 46, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "Which part of the web is used for supporting the web itself?", "options": {"A": "The center of the web.", "B": "The glue on the lines.", "C": "The anchor threads.", "D": "The capture spiral."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "C", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 12.0, "B": 12.0, "C": 66.0, "D": 10.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 3.0, "B": 4.0, "C": 92.0, "D": 2.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 23.0, "B": 21.0, "C": 36.0, "D": 20.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 66.0, "p_high": 92.0, "p_low": 36.0, "discrimination": 56.0, "p_quintiles": [95.0, 84.0, 70.0, 51.0, 28.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
{"item_uid": "指考100-english-032", "sitting": "regular", "exam_system": "指考", "year_roc": 100, "year_ce": 2011, "subject_key": "english", "subject_zh": "英文", "curriculum": "99課綱及以前", "number": 47, "join_excluded": false, "part": null, "stem": "According to the passage, which statement is true about the silk threads?", "options": {"A": "They are all sticky and extendable.", "B": "They are usually strong enough to last for a day.", "C": "They remove harmful chemicals from insects.", "D": "They are made of rare plants in the environment.\n第第第第 48 至至至至 51 題題題題為為為為題題題題組組組組\nDoctor of Philosophy, usually abbreviated as PhD or Ph.D., is an advanced academic degree\nawarded by universities. The first Doctor of Philosophy degree was awarded in Paris in 1150, but the\ndegree did not acquire its modern status until the early 19th century. The doctorate of philosophy as it\nexists today originated at Humboldt University. The German practice was later adopted by American and\nCanadian universities, eventually becoming common in large parts of the world in the 20th century.\nFor most of history, even a bachelor’s degree at a university was the privilege of a rich few, and\nmany academic staff did not hold doctorates. But as higher education expanded after the Second World\nWar, the number of PhDs increased accordingly. American universities geared up first: By 1970, America\nwas graduating half of the world’s PhDs in science and technology. Since then, America’s annual output\nof PhDs has doubled, to 64,000. Other countries are catching up. PhD production has sped up most\ndramatically in Mexico, Portugal, Italy, and Slovakia. Even Japan, where the number of young people is\nshrinking, has churned out about 46% more PhDs.\n- 6 -\n第 7 頁 100年指考\n共 7 頁 英文考科\nResearchers now warn that the supply of PhDs has far outstripped demand. America produced more\nthan 100,000 doctoral degrees between 2005 and 2009, while there were just 16,000 new professorships.\nIn research, the story is similar. Even graduates who find work outside universities may not fare all that\nwell. Statistics show that five years after receiving their degrees, more than 60% of PhDs in Slovakia and\nmore than 45% in Belgium, the Czech Republic, Germany, and Spain are still on temporary contracts.\nAbout one-third of Austria’s PhD graduates take jobs unrelated to their degrees.\nToday, top universities around the world are still picking bright students and grooming them as\npotential PhDs. After all, it isn’t in their interests to turn the smart students away: The more bright\nstudents stay at universities, the better it is for academics. But considering the oversupply of PhDs, some\npeople have already begun to wonder whether doing a PhD is a good choice for an individual."}, "option_source": null, "group_id": null, "group_passage": null, "section": null, "keys": "B", "item_flags": [], "extracted_by": "pdf", "multi_select": false, "option_dist_T": {"A": 32.0, "B": 43.0, "C": 13.0, "D": 12.0}, "option_dist_H": {"A": 37.0, "B": 59.0, "C": 2.0, "D": 2.0}, "option_dist_L": {"A": 20.0, "B": 26.0, "C": 28.0, "D": 26.0}, "omit_rate": 0.0, "p_value": 43.0, "p_high": 59.0, "p_low": 26.0, "discrimination": 33.0, "p_quintiles": [66.0, 48.0, 41.0, 34.0, 23.0], "hl_definition": "top_bottom_33pct"}
